ifElseShapeT -- the if / else / or shapes typeset wants, in an action body, on both grammars (Clay's ergonomics census,
Tony's R2, 2026-11-19). A double check of bear-trap #32: which else shapes kant reads right today.

This file is not run alone. pop.sh builds two inputs per row, one process each under an alarm: everything above this
file's stop(), then on the OLD road (incant/grammar, processCode on the first run) a call of the row's action; on the NEW
road (kantGrammar) ieNew on the row's action and on each helper the row names, then the call. A sentinel on stderr ends
each. Each row reads `stdout | R<REFUSED lines> x<exit>` and the sentinel. Every action prints the arm that ran and then
"after", so a swallowed statement or a dropped else shows as a missing line.

IE-12 is the brace arm: an action body refuses a block brace by name, AT DEFINE TIME, and the action is removed (use
indentation; that is the block shape in a body). Its define is the DEF-IE-12 lines below, added to that row's input only,
because a refusal at define time would land in every row. Pinned as read, a refusal by design, not a red row.

MEASURED 2026-11-19 (first run): every shape row, IE-1 to IE-16, reads the same on both roads and as the shape intends; no red
row, so no fixit.
Bear-trap #32's multi-statement-arm-then-else shape is right on both grammars in an action body (rows IE-3, IE-4, IE-10,
IE-11, IE-16).

IE-17 TO IE-28 are the census rows (docs/ergoCensus.md): the other spellings typeset wants, in an action body, pinned as
read. Three read as not intended and are pinned so, not cured: IE-23 (`for lp in *h` on the old road walks two items, the
star and the name, at exit 0), IE-25 (`*t["name"]` on the new road, exit 139) and IE-27 (`h == 2` with h := qa compares
the holder, not the value: "no" on both roads; `*h == 2` is the spelling).

